

Task

You maintain a music playlist as a singly linked list. Complete the three functions in playlist.c: 
append a song at the end, remove a song by exact title, and move a song one place toward the front.

Provided files

playlist.h — the Node struct and function prototypes (read-only).

main.c — the driver that reads input and calls your functions. Printing the playlist and 
freeing it are already handled here for you.

playlist.c — the file you edit.

Functions to implement

void appendSong(Node **head, const char *title)
add a song at the end of the playlist.

int removeSong(Node **head, const char *title)
remove the first song whose title matches exactly; 
return 1 if removed, 0 if no such song.

int moveUp(Node **head, const char *title) 
move the matching song one position 
toward the front; return 1 if the list changed, 0 if it was already first or not found.

Use pointer-to-pointer traversal (Node **link) so that removing or relinking the head node 
needs no special case. Each title fits in the node's title array, and titles are compared with strcmp.

Exact output strings

The driver prints all of these; your functions should not print anything themselves. Match the format 
the driver expects from your list operations:

Enter number of songs: 
Enter song 1: 
Playlist:
  - Title
Enter song title to remove: 
Removed: Title
Not found: Title
Enter song title to move up: 
Moved up: Title
No move: Title

Each playlist line is two spaces, a dash, a space, then the title.



Output:

Enter number of songs: Enter song 1: Enter song 2: Enter song 3: Playlist:
  - A
  - B
  - C
Enter song title to remove: Removed: A
Playlist:
  - B
  - C
Enter song title to move up: Moved up: C
Playlist:
  - C
  - B


Note: In your terminal the input you type appears inline after each prompt, as shown above.